    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nIn this essay, the author places the history of her family and their culinary traditions in a larger history of Sephardic Jewish migrations, and uses a food studies lens to show that hegemonic constructions of Jewishness such as the formation of\r\nAshkenazi Jewish culture in the United States, can unwittingly enact forms of cultural erasure on minority Jewish cultures like the Sephardic.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
